a route system which is near the entrance block anywhere from

222
00:19:17,721 --> 00:19:22,380
up to a half mile to the rear of the main work. Underground galleries

223
00:19:22,381 --> 00:19:25,980
containing barracks accommodations,

224
00:19:25,981 --> 00:19:29,600
a power plant capable of sustaining the ouvrage,

225
00:19:29,601 --> 00:19:34,420
and the main ammunition supply were located near these blocks.

226
00:19:34,421 --> 00:19:38,220
And then running from the roots to the crown of the tree where the combat blocks

227
00:19:38,221 --> 00:19:38,921
were

228
00:19:38,922 --> 00:19:41,900
was one long gallery and through this gallery

229
00:19:41,901 --> 00:19:46,360
There was a train called the Metro after the Paris underground system.

230
00:19:46,980 --> 00:19:48,820
And this is what a lot of people have heard about.

231
00:19:48,920 --> 00:19:50,740
There really was a train. It was electric-powered.

232
00:19:51,240 --> 00:19:54,420
And it was used primarily to haul ammunition. The troops mostly had to walk.

233
00:19:55,720 --> 00:20:00,760
The combat blocks each had smaller underground galleries connected to the main gallery.

234
00:20:01,600 --> 00:20:06,900
These galleries included secondary magazines, small rest areas for troops,

235
00:20:07,620 --> 00:20:11,700
elevator shafts that lifted ammunition and supplies to the combat blocks.

236
00:20:11,900 --> 00:20:15,420
The normal garrison area for the troops was in the rear area.

237
00:20:15,740 --> 00:20:17,720
The troops were triple bunked in most cases.

238
00:20:18,160 --> 00:20:19,240
The bunks were crammed in.

239
00:20:19,640 --> 00:20:21,660
There was also kitchens, facilities.

240
00:20:22,520 --> 00:20:26,720
Each fort had its own water supply, latrines, hospital.

241
00:20:27,340 --> 00:20:29,940
Everything needed to be self-sufficient for a month or more.

242
00:20:30,660 --> 00:20:33,360
Combat blocks were usually two-story structures,

243
00:20:33,920 --> 00:20:35,840
with weapons mounted on the upper level

244
00:20:35,841 --> 00:20:39,340
and ammunition and air filtration equipment on the lower level.

245
00:20:39,341 --> 00:20:45,780
Here is located the machinery which can raise air pressure to keep out poison gas.

246
00:20:45,781 --> 00:20:51,340
Air inside the works was kept at a higher pressure than air outside, so that gas could

247
00:20:51,341 --> 00:20:54,960
not come in through openings.

248
00:20:54,961 --> 00:21:00,340
Large filtration systems were designed to filter out any poison gas.

249
00:21:00,341 --> 00:21:04,160
Unfortunately, the plumbing didn't work quite so well.

250
00:21:04,161 --> 00:21:09,260
Drainage proved a vexing problem, as did the stench from clogged latrines.

251
00:21:09,261 --> 00:21:14,220
The overall dampness inside the line made it relatively unpleasant for those garrisoned there.

252
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:18,880
They found they had some problems, like it was too damp in many of these overages.

253
00:21:19,760 --> 00:21:26,300
And basically, what they ended up doing was putting in space heaters to warm up the place for the men,

254
00:21:26,420 --> 00:21:29,620
and it doesn't go away. It's always damp in those overages.

255
00:21:33,420 --> 00:21:38,480
Though radio communication was available, phones were more secure and reliable.

256
00:21:39,200 --> 00:21:43,340
All of the Maginot line works were connected together through an internal phone system

257
00:21:43,341 --> 00:21:49,000
that was also connected to the public phone system by telephone cables buried deep into the ground.

258
00:21:50,540 --> 00:21:54,620
Similarly, the works were hooked into France's main electricity grid,

259
00:21:55,040 --> 00:21:57,820
even though they were capable of generating their own power.

260
00:21:58,500 --> 00:22:02,380
This was a really high technology. Everything inside is electrical.

261
00:22:02,381 --> 00:22:10,300
and you must imagine that at this time in some country villages there was even no electricity

262
00:22:10,301 --> 00:22:17,940
and they had a heater, they had flowing water, they had toilets inside

263
00:22:17,941 --> 00:22:22,020
and this is really luxury and something very modern for this time.

264
00:22:23,800 --> 00:22:27,680
Ammunition, equipment and supplies were brought to the line by trucks

265
00:22:27,681 --> 00:22:31,040
or special 24-inch gauge military railroads.

266
00:22:31,041 --> 00:22:35,280
the same gauge as that used by trains inside the grosso virages.

267
00:22:36,280 --> 00:22:40,400
Diesel engines pulled small cars carrying ammunition and supplies

268
00:22:40,401 --> 00:22:42,320
into the munitions' entrance blocks,

269
00:22:42,660 --> 00:22:47,320
where they were switched over and hauled by electric locomotives in the galleries.

270
00:22:48,540 --> 00:22:51,040
The forts contained large amounts of ammunition,

271
00:22:51,600 --> 00:22:55,320
and provisions were made for supplying that rapidly to the combat blocks,

272
00:22:55,400 --> 00:22:59,080
because the combat blocks, although they were small in number and didn't have many guns,

273
00:22:59,081 --> 00:23:02,700
They had very rapid firing guns, and they could burn a lot of ammunition in a hurry.

274
00:23:03,580 --> 00:23:09,460
A main magazine near the entrance stored reserve supplies in very large crate-like containers.

275
00:23:10,180 --> 00:23:14,540
These crates could be transported by overhead monorail trolleys to the main gallery.

276
00:23:15,640 --> 00:23:21,440
To control its artillery fire, each Grosu-Virage had an integral observation post.

277
00:23:22,700 --> 00:23:26,720
Additional observation posts were located at intervals along the line.

278
00:23:26,721 --> 00:23:32,720
Each observation post had photo murals that identified all visible landscape features.

279
00:23:32,721 --> 00:23:38,720
So the observers at these observation posts could get on the phone, call back to the command post,

280
00:23:38,721 --> 00:23:43,720
and say, the Germans are at the intersection of road so-and-so and such-and-such.

281
00:23:43,721 --> 00:23:46,720
And the people at the command post would know exactly where that was,

282
00:23:46,721 --> 00:23:50,720
and they could allocate a combat block to fire on that attack.

283
00:23:50,721 --> 00:23:54,720
So again, even though there was a relatively small number of artillery pieces in the forts,

284
00:23:54,721 --> 00:23:56,720
they could be very effective.

285
00:23:56,721 --> 00:24:00,720
The line kept a low profile along the surface.

286
00:24:00,721 --> 00:24:05,720
The exception was the fixed, unretractable steel dome called a cloche,

287
00:24:05,721 --> 00:24:07,720
which means bell in French.

288
00:24:07,721 --> 00:24:13,720
Over 1,500 were built, each large enough to hold one or two men.

289
00:24:13,721 --> 00:24:19,720
The cloches served many purposes, including observation and close-in defense.

290
00:24:19,721 --> 00:24:23,720
Sitting on the surface like giant steel mushrooms,

291
00:24:23,721 --> 00:24:27,900
closes were made a very thick metal they were very strong

292
00:24:27,901 --> 00:24:31,840
armor up to 10 inches thick but it turned out that that wasn't really quite

293
00:24:31,841 --> 00:24:35,480
thick enough because the Germans came up with the 88 millimeter anti-aircraft gun

294
00:24:35,481 --> 00:24:39,140
which although it couldn't penetrate in most cases

295
00:24:39,141 --> 00:24:43,160
could certainly do enough damage to give the the people inside a real headache

296
00:24:43,161 --> 00:24:49,720
although the reinforced concrete in the Maginot line was up to 12 feet thick

297
00:24:49,721 --> 00:24:52,540
walls that made up the back of the line were much thinner,

298
00:24:53,160 --> 00:24:55,960
at four or five feet, or even less.

299
00:24:56,840 --> 00:24:58,700
But there was a good reason for this.

300
00:24:59,420 --> 00:25:04,420
And the reason this all comes about is because that's exactly the problem they had in World War I.

301
00:25:04,540 --> 00:25:07,900
The Battle of Verdun, Fort Duamont, the pride of the French forts,

302
00:25:07,960 --> 00:25:10,140
was taken by the Germans early in the campaign.

303
00:25:10,640 --> 00:25:14,840
And the French spent months trying to recapture their own fort because it was so well built.

304
00:25:16,240 --> 00:25:19,600
By the mid-30s, the bulk of the major work was completed.

305
00:25:19,720 --> 00:25:21,120
and on time.

306
00:25:23,720 --> 00:25:24,780
Quarters for the men.

307
00:25:28,180 --> 00:25:30,480
State-of-the-art command posts.

308
00:25:34,800 --> 00:25:36,140
Spotless kitchens.

309
00:25:43,780 --> 00:25:45,220
Hospital rooms.

310
00:25:47,380 --> 00:25:49,660
And, of course, weaponry.

311
00:25:49,720 --> 00:26:01,120
the French tried to keep the line a secret but this was difficult because local subcontractors

312
00:26:01,121 --> 00:26:05,500
were hired and many of the villages along the Maginot Line bounced back and forth between

313
00:26:05,501 --> 00:26:11,440
Germany and France every time the two countries signed an armistice the people involved in the

314
00:26:11,441 --> 00:26:18,040
construction of a machine line were yes local enterprises civilian and enterprises and they

315
00:26:18,041 --> 00:26:24,160
had to to hire many many workers including foreign workers because there

316
00:26:24,161 --> 00:26:30,480
was a lack of workers as construction continued the scope of the line was

317
00:26:30,481 --> 00:26:37,540
extended south along the Rhine River and west toward the English Channel but the

318
00:26:37,541 --> 00:26:41,920
funding for the extensions was not on the scale that Andre Maginot had secured

319
00:26:41,921 --> 00:26:46,040
for the original Maginot line before his death and was inadequate for the

320
00:26:46,041 --> 00:26:47,048
expanded system.

321
00:26:47,049 --> 00:26:51,460
Ironically, as the Germans began to learn more about the Maginot Line,

322
00:26:51,740 --> 00:26:54,949
the French seemed to understand it less.

323
00:26:54,950 --> 00:26:58,800
The 20-mile-long Saar Gap portion of the Maginot

324
00:26:58,801 --> 00:27:03,960
Line was to be fortified primarily with a series of dands which would flood this low-lying area.

325
00:27:05,120 --> 00:27:09,157
Severe drought, and not the enemy, ultimately undid this barrier.

326
00:27:09,158 --> 00:27:11,020
The Maginot Line will continue

327
00:27:11,021 --> 00:27:12,300
on Modern Marvel.

328
00:27:16,040 --> 00:27:21,260
We now return to the Maginot Line on Modern Marvels.

329
00:27:23,960 --> 00:27:30,700
After the Treaty of Versailles, the French hoped that a demilitarized Rhineland would act as a buffer to any German attack.

330
00:27:32,260 --> 00:27:40,640
The French, in fact, tried to create a Rhine Republic, a separate German state that was a satellite state to France,

331
00:27:41,080 --> 00:27:43,480
but America wouldn't go along with it in 1919.

332
00:27:43,481 --> 00:27:49,400
And so this was the compromise, the militarily occupied Rhineland.

333
00:27:50,180 --> 00:27:57,760
The Rhineland was to be occupied until 1936, giving France the breathing space it needed to recover from the war.

334
00:27:58,280 --> 00:28:02,020
But once again, France was disappointed by its allies.

335
00:28:03,660 --> 00:28:09,540
As it actually turned out, the Rhineland was evacuated five years ahead of schedule,

336
00:28:09,541 --> 00:28:16,720
in part because of the diplomatic negotiations that went on in mid-decade about other aspects of the Versailles Treaty.

337
00:28:18,400 --> 00:28:23,640
In 1936, as the initial construction effort on the Maginot Line was nearing completion,

338
00:28:24,320 --> 00:28:26,760
Hitler gave France a dress rehearsal for war.

339
00:28:27,660 --> 00:28:33,220
Hitler's first foreign policy move was to reoccupy the Rhineland with the new army that he was building.

340
00:28:33,221 --> 00:28:38,720
And some people have said if France at that point had sent troops,

341
00:28:39,340 --> 00:28:44,280
that might have stopped Hitler very early on and shown him his limitations.

342
00:28:45,260 --> 00:28:48,560
But France was without allies. Nobody wanted to help France.

343
00:28:48,920 --> 00:28:52,920
It didn't have the will to fight, and so it just let it go.

344
00:28:54,300 --> 00:28:58,220
France reacted to the Rhineland crisis even before Hitler's reoccupation

345
00:28:58,860 --> 00:29:01,320
with a plan to extend the Maginot Line.

346
00:29:02,240 --> 00:29:05,640
Unsure of its allies and unable to muster a sizable army,

347
00:29:06,160 --> 00:29:09,360
the French began to see the Maginot line and supporting works

348
00:29:09,361 --> 00:29:12,200
as their savior in any new conflict with Germany,

349
00:29:12,840 --> 00:29:14,920
a barrier they could hide behind.

350
00:29:16,400 --> 00:29:22,420
By the late 1930s, I think France was so much into this Maginot mentality

351
00:29:22,421 --> 00:29:24,520
and in a lot of ways even complacency,

352
00:29:24,680 --> 00:29:28,140
that the French media had really played up the Maginot line

353
00:29:28,141 --> 00:29:31,700
and that the Maginot Line was going to solve all problems,

354
00:29:31,800 --> 00:29:34,280
that the Germans would never be able to get past the fortifications.

355
00:29:35,340 --> 00:29:38,580
Other events were taking place in the world of politics

356
00:29:38,581 --> 00:29:42,480
that would have an impact on the construction and usefulness of the Maginot Line.

357
00:29:45,160 --> 00:29:49,040
France had always assumed Belgium would be its ally to the north

358
00:29:49,041 --> 00:29:52,200
and thus hadn't extended the line to the English Channel.

359
00:29:53,220 --> 00:29:57,180
In fact, construction of the line had implicitly anticipated this partnership,

360
00:29:57,181 --> 00:30:01,580
allowing the French to concentrate their forces away from the Maginot Line

361
00:30:01,581 --> 00:30:04,320
and towards wherever an attack was most likely.

362
00:30:05,760 --> 00:30:08,040
Now, where did they want to concentrate them?

363
00:30:08,460 --> 00:30:11,560
They wanted to concentrate them on the northern frontier.

364
00:30:12,740 --> 00:30:16,400
And for a good long while, what they really would have liked

365
00:30:16,401 --> 00:30:21,060
is to concentrate them and to cooperate with the Belgians

366
00:30:21,061 --> 00:30:25,900
so if there was a next war, it wouldn't be fought in France, it would be fought in Belgium.

367
00:30:25,901 --> 00:30:31,960
And after the Germans remilitarized the Rhineland in 1936, the Belgians backed out.

368
00:30:32,560 --> 00:30:39,800
Why should they cooperate with the French in using their territory as the main theater of operations?

369
00:30:40,300 --> 00:30:42,580
They were in a very, very difficult position.

370
00:30:43,820 --> 00:30:48,160
In 1936, Belgium declared its permanent neutrality.

371
00:30:48,760 --> 00:30:52,880
And suddenly, France had a huge exposed flank all along its border with Belgium.

372
00:30:53,680 --> 00:30:56,500
There wasn't enough money or enough time to do anything about it,

373
00:30:56,960 --> 00:30:58,340
but the French tried anyway.

374
00:30:59,520 --> 00:31:02,120
They began something known as the New Fronts.

375
00:31:02,121 --> 00:31:07,920
And these New Fronts, which the English translate into an extension of the Maginot Line,

376
00:31:08,200 --> 00:31:15,340
and they extended the Maginot Line at this point from the vicinity of Montmédi towards Villy.

377
00:31:15,920 --> 00:31:19,440
And this Maginot extension only had a few heavy fortifications in it,

378
00:31:19,441 --> 00:31:22,900
and there was still a slight gap between Montmagny and Longyong.

379
00:31:24,300 --> 00:31:28,560
They were built in the same basic style, the same basic technique,

380
00:31:28,660 --> 00:31:29,760
but they weren't nearly as strong.

381
00:31:29,900 --> 00:31:32,540
They had a lot less artillery, the works were farther apart,

382
00:31:32,760 --> 00:31:35,740
and it was definitely not up to the standard of the marginal line itself,

383
00:31:35,820 --> 00:31:36,900
or the marginal line proper.

384
00:31:38,660 --> 00:31:41,600
Beyond the dense Ardenne forest toward Dunkirk,

385
00:31:42,000 --> 00:31:44,360
the landscape posed insurmountable problems.

386
00:31:45,120 --> 00:31:48,180
The region is heavily industrialized along the border with Belgium.

387
00:31:48,181 --> 00:31:55,180
In addition, the land close to the coast has a high water table, making underground galleries infeasible.

388
00:31:55,181 --> 00:32:00,180
Finally, no natural barriers exist along this flat plain.

389
00:32:00,181 --> 00:32:06,180
France decided it would deploy soldiers along that border as a defense.

390
00:32:06,181 --> 00:32:10,180
At the same time that France began construction of the Maginot Line,

391
00:32:10,181 --> 00:32:16,180
it began an extensive program of fortification construction in the Alps, along the border with Italy.

392
00:32:18,180 --> 00:32:22,120
The difficult landscape provided better defensive positions.

393
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:27,500
Although these fortifications were not necessarily as dense as those in the Maginot line proper,

394
00:32:28,000 --> 00:32:33,580
they were similarly built, and their location often gave them a considerable advantage over an invading enemy.

395
00:32:34,660 --> 00:32:39,520
The combat blocks in the Alps will have casements that will actually,

396
00:32:39,960 --> 00:32:43,080
the artillery will face towards the direction of the enemy.

397
00:32:43,660 --> 00:32:46,160
And this is because there was no other way to do it.

398
00:32:46,161 --> 00:32:50,520
It was too expensive to put in turrets, and this was considered the most effective when building in the mountains.

399
00:32:51,020 --> 00:32:57,440
So many of these combat casements will be reinforced so that they can meet the enemy.

400
00:32:59,440 --> 00:33:04,160
The Little Maginot Line, as the Alpine defenses came to be known, was strong.

401
00:33:04,760 --> 00:33:07,880
But fortifications are only one part of a country's security.

402
00:33:08,720 --> 00:33:14,760
As French Armed Services enlistment hit an all-time low, no one seemed to be looking for other options.

403
00:33:14,761 --> 00:33:23,720
At this point, the French army, the military command, was run by old men who did not have a lot of new ideas.

404
00:33:23,980 --> 00:33:26,760
They didn't take into consideration things like the tank.

405
00:33:28,500 --> 00:33:34,100
They didn't really put a lot of money into experimenting with armored forces the way the Germans were.

406
00:33:34,580 --> 00:33:36,400
They also didn't build up their air force.

407
00:33:36,660 --> 00:33:41,900
They didn't, they weren't, you know, these were new technologies that they really weren't interested in.

408
00:33:41,901 --> 00:33:47,480
I think it's fair to say that there was a mindset about not trying these new technologies,

409
00:33:47,680 --> 00:33:51,840
not trying to go on the offensive, and just making sure that France would not be attacked again.

410
00:33:52,800 --> 00:33:59,080
To ensure this, the French spread propaganda indicating the Maginot Line was much stronger than it was,

411
00:33:59,460 --> 00:34:03,060
that it could deflect any German attack, that it was invincible.

412
00:34:03,700 --> 00:34:06,820
One of the myths that was quite prevalent, particularly in the early days,

413
00:34:06,821 --> 00:34:10,420
was that the Maginot Line is connected entirely together underground

414
00:34:10,421 --> 00:34:12,920
by this massive underground rail network.

415
00:34:13,400 --> 00:34:15,460
There were some really fantastic stories that appeared

416
00:34:15,461 --> 00:34:17,940
in some of the British newspapers and some illustrations

417
00:34:17,941 --> 00:34:21,160
that bore absolutely no relation to reality.

418
00:34:23,480 --> 00:34:26,520
Unfortunately, the propaganda worked better to fool the French

419
00:34:26,521 --> 00:34:28,700
into thinking the Maginot Line was unbreachable

420
00:34:28,701 --> 00:34:31,440
than it did to convince the Germans that it was.

421
00:34:32,480 --> 00:34:36,480
Good propaganda is propaganda that people actually want to believe.

422
00:34:36,481 --> 00:34:40,040
and if you tell it to them often enough, they will end up believing it.

423
00:34:40,600 --> 00:34:43,580
That was part of the reason for the defeatism

424
00:34:43,581 --> 00:34:49,540
which was rampant in French society in the 1930s

425
00:34:49,541 --> 00:34:54,020
and which indirectly led to a sapping of military morale.

426
00:34:55,800 --> 00:34:58,420
Newsreel footage praising the strength of the line

427
00:34:58,421 --> 00:35:02,320
also did a better job of lulling the French into a false sense of security

428
00:35:02,321 --> 00:35:04,860
than scaring the Germans away from an attack.

429
00:35:04,861 --> 00:35:12,140
Though not fooled by the propaganda, the Germans knew the line was a formidable defense.

430
00:35:12,141 --> 00:35:17,080
They decided upon a plan to neutralize the Maginot Line, even as they began gobbling

431
00:35:17,081 --> 00:35:19,540
up their neighbors.

432
00:35:19,541 --> 00:35:23,400
Hitler responded to the Maginot Line with a defensive line of his own.

433
00:35:23,401 --> 00:35:28,520
A construction of his Westval was never completed.

434
00:35:28,521 --> 00:35:29,780
Modern Marvels will return.

435
00:35:34,360 --> 00:35:38,600
We now return to the Maginot Line on Modern Marvels.

436
00:35:41,100 --> 00:35:47,420
In 1939, the border between France and Germany was a taut line stretched across Europe.

437
00:35:48,500 --> 00:35:52,680
Hitler had first tested the waters of world opinion when he reoccupied the Rhineland.

438
00:35:52,681 --> 00:35:58,720
And thanks to a policy of appeasement, particularly from England, he found the waters warm.

439
00:35:59,260 --> 00:36:02,900
As the decade drew to a close, so did any chance for peace.

440
00:36:03,640 --> 00:36:08,680
Hitler's primary purpose in the 30s was to reverse the Versailles Treaty,

441
00:36:09,460 --> 00:36:11,740
step by step, territory by territory.

442
00:36:13,200 --> 00:36:16,360
And after he had strengthened Germany in that way,

443
00:36:17,020 --> 00:36:20,880
he was going to launch his attack on the east.

444
00:36:20,881 --> 00:36:26,880
His argument again that he's just trying to reunite German-speaking peoples with Germany,

445
00:36:26,881 --> 00:36:31,880
and this of course leads to the very famous Munich conference with Neville Chamberlain,

446
00:36:31,881 --> 00:36:36,880
the British Prime Minister, claiming we have peace in our time, and that Hitler is done.

447
00:36:36,881 --> 00:36:41,880
He just wanted to get all German-speaking peoples together, and now he will be satisfied.

448
00:36:41,881 --> 00:36:46,880
We regard the agreement signed last night and the Anglo-German naval agreement

449
00:36:46,881 --> 00:36:54,000
agreement as symbolic of the desire of our two peoples never to go to war with one another again

450
00:36:55,760 --> 00:37:01,360
and then hitler turns around in 1939 and invades poland with this invasion

451
00:37:01,361 --> 00:37:06,240
england and france finally declared war on germany on september 3rd 1939

452
00:37:09,280 --> 00:37:15,520
britain went to war against germany today 25 years and 30 days from the time she entered

453
00:37:15,521 --> 00:37:18,199
the war of 1914 against the same enemy.

454
00:37:18,200 --> 00:37:22,320
France is expected to follow suit within the next few hours.

455
00:37:23,200 --> 00:37:28,240
The Maginot Line was put on full alert and troops were deployed along its entire length.

456
00:37:29,840 --> 00:37:31,974
In fact, France invaded Germany first.

457
00:37:31,975 --> 00:37:35,120
Although it made limited excursions just over the German

458
00:37:35,121 --> 00:37:40,063
border, no serious offensive effort was ever considered.

459
00:37:40,064 --> 00:37:42,800
The most they ever did was send

460
00:37:42,801 --> 00:37:46,500
maybe a herd of sheep across the frontier to see where the landmines were.

461
00:37:48,000 --> 00:37:50,180
And so nothing happened, no shooting.

462
00:37:50,920 --> 00:37:53,480
And so rather than it being called the Blitzkrieg,

463
00:37:53,640 --> 00:37:56,860
it was called the Sitzkrieg, meaning the sitting war,

464
00:37:57,320 --> 00:37:59,500
or in English we called it the phony war.

465
00:38:00,100 --> 00:38:03,040
This was fine with the French, who had no choice now

466
00:38:03,041 --> 00:38:05,080
but to put their faith in the Maginot line.

467
00:38:05,081 --> 00:38:15,420
In the northeast, the Maginot line proper bristled with strong defenses.

468
00:38:16,240 --> 00:38:19,120
In the high Alps, forts kept watch along the border with Italy.

469
00:38:20,020 --> 00:38:24,780
The French anticipated deploying the bulk of their troops along the border between Belgium and France.

470
00:38:25,000 --> 00:38:30,140
The only gap in the defenses was in the heavily wooded area known as the Ardennes Forest.

471
00:38:31,520 --> 00:38:34,780
The French believed that no modern army could invade through the Ardennes.

472
00:38:35,080 --> 00:38:37,400
so they didn't worry very much about defending it.

473
00:38:38,700 --> 00:38:43,380
But the Germans realized the Maginot Line was a formidable defense best avoided.

474
00:38:44,220 --> 00:38:45,200
And they did.

475
00:38:46,360 --> 00:38:50,740
The Ardennes Forest would be no barrier to the modern German blitzkrieg assault.

476
00:38:51,980 --> 00:38:55,700
The Maginot Line forced the enemy to attack France's weakest point.

477
00:38:56,800 --> 00:39:00,860
And this proved fatal for France, as the Germans pushed through,

478
00:39:01,200 --> 00:39:04,480
using tanks and airplanes in ways the French hadn't anticipated.

479
00:39:05,080 --> 00:39:12,880
They went through that area and wrapped up the French and British armies and the Belgian armies and the Dutch armies.

480
00:39:13,380 --> 00:39:16,740
And then they prepared to swing down into France.

481
00:39:16,741 --> 00:39:25,240
And what they did was a great turning movement, starting along the coast and swinging around and then coming around in to attack the marginal line from the rear.

482
00:39:35,080 --> 00:39:49,080
For the most part, when the Germans did attack the Maginot Line, they were unsuccessful.

483
00:39:52,720 --> 00:39:59,040
With the combat block standing up well to bombardment by the heaviest artillery the Germans could bring into play.

484
00:40:02,500 --> 00:40:04,200
But there were exceptions.

485
00:40:05,080 --> 00:40:15,540
The petite ouvrage of La Ferté at the far western end of the Maginot Line extension

486
00:40:15,541 --> 00:40:17,960
did fall to the Germans.

487
00:40:17,961 --> 00:40:23,760
The Germans were able to take that position because the Maginot extension had never been

488
00:40:23,761 --> 00:40:26,860
designed like the rest of the Maginot Line proper.

489
00:40:26,861 --> 00:40:30,960
They'd done too much economizing and the ouvrages could not give the same kind of support they

490
00:40:30,961 --> 00:40:33,740
They couldn't imagine line proper because of the distance between them.

491
00:40:34,740 --> 00:40:38,840
The Germans marched into Paris on June 14th, 1940,

492
00:40:39,420 --> 00:40:43,180
and seized the heart of France without ever breaking through the Maginot Line.

493
00:40:44,520 --> 00:40:48,900
The southern forces were in disarray, chaos reigned,

494
00:40:49,380 --> 00:40:53,240
and the French high command surrendered.

495
00:40:53,440 --> 00:40:57,400
This is William L. Shirer speaking from the forest of Compiègne,

496
00:40:57,401 --> 00:41:02,480
where Adolf Hitler today is handing his armistice terms to France.

497
00:41:03,200 --> 00:41:08,400
It is 3.15 p.m. Adolf Hitler strides slowly toward the little clearing.

498
00:41:09,260 --> 00:41:14,120
I can see his face, this grave, solemn, yet brimming with revenge.

499
00:41:15,600 --> 00:41:18,740
Within weeks of the German invasion, France was defeated.

500
00:41:22,700 --> 00:41:24,520
Hitler was in Paris.

501
00:41:24,521 --> 00:41:30,620
The bulk of the troops along much of the Maginot Line hardly heard a gun fire.

502
00:41:31,720 --> 00:41:34,500
Word came down that France had capitulated.

503
00:41:35,280 --> 00:41:39,720
Undefeated, the troops surrendered the Maginot Line only to become prisoners of war.

504
00:41:40,600 --> 00:41:44,620
Soon, the Maginot Line became a storage depot for German military equipment.

505
00:41:45,300 --> 00:41:49,620
The world was stunned at how quickly France collapsed under the German juggernaut.

506
00:41:49,621 --> 00:42:00,740
Built into much of the criticism is the expectation that the French were in a position single-handedly to have forestall the Germans and prevented a Second World War.

507
00:42:01,240 --> 00:42:10,280
And I think that is a gross distortion of what France's position was, what its capabilities were.

508
00:42:11,740 --> 00:42:15,560
Ironically, the Maginot Line sometimes takes heavier flack today.

509
00:42:15,561 --> 00:42:20,660
than it ever got from Germany during the war.

510
00:42:21,320 --> 00:42:23,500
No matter how wonderful a bulwark you built,

511
00:42:24,040 --> 00:42:27,580
if it doesn't do what it's supposed to do is to keep the enemy out,

512
00:42:27,920 --> 00:42:30,320
then it's in the wrong place or it's the wrong bulwark.

513
00:42:31,000 --> 00:42:35,020
Klausowitz, the great 19th century German theorist of war,

514
00:42:35,021 --> 00:42:39,180
said, if you entrench yourself behind strong fortifications,

515
00:42:39,420 --> 00:42:42,000
you compel the enemy to seek a solution elsewhere.

516
00:42:42,220 --> 00:42:44,040
And that, of course, is just what the Wiley Hun did.

517
00:42:44,300 --> 00:42:45,100
Others disagree.

518
00:42:45,560 --> 00:42:49,060
The Maginot Line was going to serve as a shield as it was intended for.

519
00:42:49,340 --> 00:42:52,820
That's what they wanted, that's what they got, that's what it did.

520
00:42:53,320 --> 00:42:55,960
The Maginot Line protected the border with Germany.

521
00:42:55,961 --> 00:43:02,760
I wouldn't say the line was a failure so much as the whole French military operation in 1940 was a failure.

522
00:43:03,360 --> 00:43:05,720
And I think that's where the emphasis would fall.

523
00:43:07,240 --> 00:43:12,140
Though the Maginot Line was the most technologically sophisticated fortification built,

524
00:43:12,480 --> 00:43:15,120
ever-advancing technology ultimately defeated it.

525
00:43:15,560 --> 00:43:20,100
Fortifications like the Maginot Line are obsolete because of air power.

526
00:43:20,640 --> 00:43:31,340
Once you can fly over them easily, they're not going to be able to stop any high-flying airplanes carrying whatever bombs it may be.

527
00:43:31,480 --> 00:43:33,560
Certainly after World War II, nuclear bombs.

528
00:43:34,020 --> 00:43:39,560
How would the Maginot Line possibly deflect a nuclear attack? It can't.

529
00:43:39,820 --> 00:43:43,820
In the long run, that may be one of the biggest failures in the Maginot Line.

530
00:43:43,821 --> 00:43:48,720
It may have engendered this false sense of security on the part of the French,

531
00:43:49,260 --> 00:43:56,060
that perhaps they would have been better off to take the money they had spent on the Maginot Line

532
00:43:56,061 --> 00:44:00,220
and to spend that money on improving their army, improving their training.

533
00:44:01,200 --> 00:44:06,980
By the close of World War II, the age of the fortified defensive barrier had ended.

534
00:44:07,860 --> 00:44:12,980
In the nuclear age, no serious attempt has been made to build a fortress around a country.

535
00:44:13,820 --> 00:44:26,760
And so the Maginot Line still wanders helter-skelter through the eastern fields of France, invaded only by tourists, technological high point and low point all at once.

